WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
I'm tomorrow saying reading for reviewer  Richard weakling Ghost Recon breakpoint  is uneven and conflicted it offers a  near identical core gameplay loop of  open-world espionage and shooting as  wildlands did but it's also a messy  hodgepodge of disparate ideas shoehorned  in half-baked and our place the addition  of loot and an ever increasing gear  score fits into the standard template of  Ubisoft recent open-world games  breakpoint includes these light RPG  mechanics but the implementation of its  lucre and feels like a severe  afterthought there are numerous pieces  of armor to find and equip as you  explore the fictional island of Aurora  the numbers attached to each one will  raise or lower your gear score but the  effect this has on gameplay is entirely  inconsequential rare loot might include  small buffs yet the effects of these  buffs are also negligible a level 5  beanie offers as much protection as a  level 75 helmet so these numbers only  exist to raise a gear score that's  nothing more than a flimsy  representation of your progress it's  difficult to care when there are no  tangible benefits to picking one piece  of armor over another choosing which  weapon to roll with requires slightly  more deliberation although this is  mainly due to your preference for  specific weapon types as opposed to the  number attached to each firearms  function similar to armor with rare  weapons receiving minuscule busts two  aspects like reload speed and recoil  reduction again the impact these stats  have on the gameplay is paltry at best  especially because shooting and  breakpoint is still geared towards  landing headshots for instant kills  weapons feel impactful as a result of  this successfully capturing the rush of  being an elite Special Ops soldier that  can take out four or five enemy  combatants in a matter of seconds but  this also means that the rarity of  weapons and the gear score attached to  them is ultimately meaningless you can  wander into an area recommended for  players with a gear score of 140 with a  significantly lower score and still kill  every enemy without breaking a sweat the  amount of freedom will be commendable if  it didn't shine a derisive light on how  shallow the RPG mechanics are break  points paper-thin survival mechanics are  similarly underdeveloped hinting at a  tense experience that never comes to  fruition you can carry a floss that you  can refill in lakes rivers and even in  someone's backyard swimming pool for  that sweet taste of chlorine water is  used to replenish any law stamina you've  misplaced by overexerting usually by  rolling down a hillside because Aurora  is nearly bereft of flat ground as a  result traversing on foot revolves  around spending a lot of time sliding  down undulating slopes this quickly  drains your stamina sending you into an  uncontrollable roll that inflicts damage  with each nick and bump health  regenerates over time but if you suffer  either a minor or major injury and don't  want to hobble everywhere you need to  use a syringe for instant pain relief or  spend longer wrapping yourself up in  bandages syringes are finite yet you  have an infinite supply of bandages that  almost makes the mechanic moot the  gameplay loop is almost identical to  wild lenses you send a drone into the  sky survey an enemy base and mark  targets before infiltrating in whatever  manner you see fit navigating through a  heavily fortified compound without being  seen is still inherently satisfying each  one is usually designed in a way so that  there are a number of enemies obscured  from your drones vision you might be  able to pick off a handful of God's from  a distance using a silent sniper rifle  but at some point you'll have to enter  and find the rest  the only thing impeding your stealthy  espionage is the fact that you can't  move sideways while prone instead you  end up with these awkward animations  because you can only turn at right  angles taking cover is overly cumbersome  too you do it automatically but what the  game deems as cover is inconsistent from  one low wall to the next and even if you  do manage to get behind an object  whether you can shoe over it or not is  another question though this will be a  bigger problem if the AI were the least  bit competent enemies in break point are  to the point where playing on the  highest difficulty doesn't present a  significantly harder challenge  bottlenecks like corridors and doorways  are by far the worst enemy sit down one  end of a straight corridor and it  doesn't take long for the bodies to pile  up you can even shoot the ground at the  entrance to a base and kill each enemy  who appears to investigate factoring the  disappointing fact that enemies don't so  much as flinch when getting shot in the  body and none of this is conducive to an  enjoyable combat experience shooting  other players in the ghost war PvP mode  fares better since real people tend to  have their wits about them compared to  the AI unlike wildlands breakpoint  cleverly unifies progression across both  single and multiplayer all of your  weapons and skills carry over and the  rewards you unlock can be bought back  into single-player - the best matches in  ghost war are tense Affairs especially  since you only have a single life unless  a teammate can perform a successful  revive the issue with break points  cumbersome cover mechanic an awkward  prone movement are only exacerbated in  multiplayer however it can also be  difficult just getting into a match of  ghosts or due to relatively frequent  server issues break point is an always  online game even if you're playing alone  in single-player the servers have run  into a few problems since the game's  full release and it's incredibly  frustrating to be kicked back to the  main menu and have to restart a mission  all over again when you're not even  engaging with the multiplayer portion of  the game if you want to do so and the  servers are running smoothly and you can  get some like-minded friends together  there's definitely some fun to be had in  break points four-person co-op as  silently clearing a base of enemies is  more gratifying with other people  there is however some dissonance between  co-op and the story painting you as a  lone soldier although this is much more  egregious in breakpoint social hub you  can play the whole game solo but mission  givers all hang out in this homely cave  where you'll also find 50 or so other  players your character is literally  called Nomad and yet you're in a space  with a bunch of other nomads all  standing around the same NPC like it's  an MMO and the story's not great either  way Jon Bernthal elevates every scene  he's in chewing up the scenery to  deliver simmering monologues befitting  of a villain with a dubious moral code  the writing is mostly cheesy though with  some flat voice acting and predictable  twists the social hub seems to exist to  guide you towards breakpoints myriad  microtransactions you can buy tattoos  shirts masks hats weapons vehicles and  more purchasing in-game money also comes  in denominations that ensure you're  always spending more than you need you  don't have to engage with any of this  stuff and it's easy enough to ignore but  this micro transaction structure is  predatory by design it would make sense  if the addition of loot were in the  service of guiding people to spend real  money on better guns but even then the  stats are so meaningless it will take a  lot of convincing there's some  surprising fun to be had stealthily  infiltrating enemy compounds and playing  with friends but breakpoint is still a  generic and distinctly subpar game it's  essentially every Ubisoft open-world  game rolled into one  failing to excel in any one area or  establish its own identity breakpoint is  a messy confused game and a ghost of the  series is former self  [Music]

